# Elysia-Harness: the repository around a vendored DSH checkout.
#
# `dsh/` is a plain folder holding DSH source. Its own ignore rules arrive with
# the source, so this file covers what this repository adds on top of it.
#
# Two rules run through the whole file:
#   - track what a clone needs to reproduce the setup (profile definitions,
#     examples of machine-local files);
#   - ignore what a run writes or what describes one machine.

# ── Build output and dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reproducible from dsh/pnpm-lock.yaml plus a build.
**/node_modules/
dsh/**/lib/
plugins/*/lib/
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.pnpm-store/
**/.cache/
**/coverage/
dsh/apps/web/dist/
dsh/.dsh-build/
dsh/.dsh-report/
# The plugins are importers in dsh/pnpm-lock.yaml, so that workspace lockfile
# already pins them. One written inside a plugin is the leftover of running
# `pnpm install` in the wrong directory, not a second source of truth.
plugins/*/pnpm-lock.yaml

# ── Per-surface harness homes ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything a surface writes at runtime.
homes/*/sessions/
homes/*/storages/
homes/*/logs/
homes/*/cache/
homes/*/attachments/
homes/*/themes/
homes/*/agents/
homes/*/requests/
homes/*/.anonymous-user-id
homes/*/remote-web-ui-*.json
homes/*/remote-web-ui-registry/

# Credentials never enter history. The example shows the file's shape.
homes/*/.credentials.yaml
!homes*/.credentials.example.yaml

# Environment files are operator-local too; only examples are tracked.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.*.example

# The prompt bodies are the subject's own, not repository content. A surface
# symlinks them in from wherever they actually live.
homes/*/standing-prompts

# The fixed main-session cwd alias and prompt revision backups are local data.
homes/web/conscious-mind
homes/*/standing-prompt-backups/

# ── Machine-local model configuration ───────────────────────────────────────
# These are operator-local: the home layer may be empty when the tracked profile
# patch owns model providers, and the shared file is a machine-specific source
# for surfaces that still use it. Neither belongs in history.
homes/*/cordis.patch.yml
homes/shared/models.patch.yml
# The example is the contract: a clone reads it to build its own real file.
!homes/shared/models.patch.example.yml

# ── Local instruction overlay ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# A machine's private session context: DSH injects `AGENTS.local.md` (and
# `CLAUDE.local.md`) automatically, but they must never enter history.
**/AGENTS.local.md
**/CLAUDE.local.md

# ── Profile internals ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A profile's definition (package.json, cordis.patch.yml) is tracked: it is the
# composition this repository exists to describe. What pnpm and the loader
# generate beside it is not.
homes/*/profiles/*/node_modules/
homes/*/profiles/*/cordis.yml

# The shared dependency tree is the exception: it pins the upstream versions the
# plugin stack is built against, so its lockfile is the reproducible artifact
# every profile links to. A profile's own lockfile is generated and stays out.
homes/*/profiles/*/pnpm-lock.yaml
!homes/*/profiles/_core/pnpm-lock.yaml
homes/*/profiles/*/.dsh-module-fallback/
homes/*/profiles/*/.plugin-manager/

# ── dsh-tui's own preference directory ──────────────────────────────────────
# dsh-tui keeps UI preferences and history under a hardcoded $HOME/.dsh-tui;
# the launchers point HOME at a surface's home so that tree lands here. Ship an
# example of the preferences; ignore the real file and everything a run writes.
homes/*/.dsh-tui/*
!homes/*/.dsh-tui/settings.example.json

# ── Vendored third-party plugins ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kept outside dsh/ so an upstream update cannot touch them. Everything here is
# tracked, including their lib/ directories: unlike the packages under dsh/,
# these hold hand-written source rather than compiler output. The blanket
# dsh/**/lib/ rule above deliberately does not reach here.
vendor/*/node_modules/

# ── Personal Elysium data mirror ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The real server data copy is local-only. Only the sanitized skeleton under
# data/example is tracked.
data/elysia/

# ── Scratch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
*.orig
*.bak
# Local staged copies used by subconscious refactor verification.
plugins/subconscious-intake-check/
plugins/subconscious-tools-work/

tmp/
.DS_Store

# ── Runtime state the surfaces create for themselves ────────────────────────
# Codex / Claude backends, agent presets, and the per-profile node_modules
# symlink. None of it is reproducible from the repository; all of it is written
# by a run.
homes/*/.codex/
homes/*/.claude/
homes/*/.agent-presets/
homes/*/profiles/*/node_modules

# ── 两人在服务器上的对接区 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One machine's current hand-off state between the two people working on it.
# Durable conclusions belong in docs/; these notes can be deleted once handled.
handoff/

# Local market indexes and retained session backups are runtime state.
homes/*/profiles/*/.dsh-market/
# The market loader also materialises installed bundles beside the home, one
# directory per surface; it is downloaded state, not a source of the build.
homes/*/dsh-market/
homes/*/session-backups/

# Upstream livestream operational settings include a private stage capability.
homes/*/dsh-livestream/

# Local Ayla login and refresh state.
homes/*/ayla-app/

# ── TypeScript emit beside the vendored sources ─────────────────────────────
# Several packages under dsh/ compile in place: `tsc -b` writes x.js,
# x.js.map, x.d.ts and x.d.ts.map next to x.ts, and a single full build left
# ~300 of them untracked. Only those four shapes are ignored here. The
# hand-written ambient declarations are real source, so each one is re-included
# by name — a new ambient declaration belongs on this list.
dsh/**/src/**/*.js
dsh/**/src/**/*.js.map
dsh/**/src/**/*.d.ts
dsh/**/src/**/*.d.ts.map
!dsh/**/src/**/asset-imports.d.ts
!dsh/**/src/**/assets.d.ts
!dsh/**/src/**/css-modules.d.ts
!dsh/**/src/**/ripgrep.d.ts
!dsh/**/src/**/styles.d.ts
!dsh/**/src/**/turndown-plugin-gfm.d.ts
!dsh/**/src/**/use-sync-external-store.d.ts
!dsh/**/src/**/vite-env.d.ts

# ── Breeze C++ review ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The unpacked checkout is ignored; the tarball beside it is deliberately not.
# 5.9 MB once buys the only exact pin there is: the tarball carries no git
# metadata, so nothing else records which upstream snapshot was benchmarked.
#
# The checkout itself cannot be tracked either file-by-file or as submodules.
# Breeze-TTS-2.cpp declares ggml and shine as submodules, and their .git
# pointers address `.git/modules/...` of a standalone clone; inside this
# repository those pointers dangle, git falls back to reporting all ~2 400
# vendored files as ordinary untracked ones, and the build-cuda* trees add
# another 154 MB on top.
#
# Unpack and de-submodule where a build needs the tree (`diff -r` against the
# tarball is the check that the checkout is still the pinned one):
#   tar xzf breeze-realtime-review/breeze-tts-2-cpp-source.tar.gz \
#     -C breeze-realtime-review/
#   rm -f breeze-realtime-review/Breeze-TTS-2.cpp/third_party/{ggml,shine}/.git
breeze-realtime-review/Breeze-TTS-2.cpp/

# ── One machine's listening and measurement output ──────────────────────────
# A .wav only proves something was played; the .capture.json beside it carries
# the text, seed and duration, and that is what a later comparison reads, so the
# JSON stays tracked. The benchmark runner's raw per-chunk results and the
# scratch from unpacking a third-party package are the same kind of leftover:
# conclusions belong in docs/report/, not in history.
breeze-realtime-review/**/*.wav
breeze-realtime-benchmark/
modsearch-review/

# Ad-hoc scripts that poke one host's request archive; they hardcode that host's
# absolute paths. A diagnostic that outlives its run belongs in scripts/ with the
# paths taken from the environment.
homes/*/*.mjs

# ── Build scratch and one-off backups ───────────────────────────────────────
# A CMake module copied beside the project that used it is build scratch, not
# source. Same for the hand-made .bak files left behind when a profile was
# edited in place: the commit history is the real backup.
breeze-realtime-review/FindCUDAToolkit.cmake
breeze-realtime-review/results/
*.bak-*
*.bak
.backup-*/

# ── Browser-automation run artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────
# A Playwright MCP session started from the repo root drops its console log and
# page snapshot here. They describe one run of one page, not anything the build
# reads, so they stay out of history.
.playwright-mcp/
